  [1mClassification: bounded[0m — the login flow already exists here and this is a one-file change, so I'll present a short
  design in chat rather than write a spec. Say the word if you want the heavier treatment.

  One thing blocks me from just doing it: [1mthere is no [38;5;153muserId[39m anywhere in this repo.[0m The form collects only [38;5;153musername[39m and
  [38;5;153mpassword[39m ([38;5;153mapp.js:19-20[39m), and [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is a stub that fabricates its response. So "add a userId parameter" needs a
  source for the value, and the choices differ in what you're stuck with afterward:

  - [1mClient-generated attempt ID[0m ([38;5;153mcrypto.randomUUID()[39m at the call site) — buys you a correlation ID to tie a login
    attempt to later requests, costs nothing on the backend, but it does [3mnot[0m identify a person; it's per-attempt, not
    per-user. Cheap to change later.
  - [1mCaller-supplied real user identifier[0m — actually identifies who logged in, but nothing in this repo produces one
    pre-authentication. You'd be committing to a new identity source (page context, tenant config, or a lookup) that
    doesn't exist yet. That's a new subsystem, and it would re-classify this as architectural.
  - [1mNo new parameter; track on the existing [38;5;153musername[0m — [38;5;153musername[39m already answers "who logged in," so the tracking goes
    around the call rather than into its signature. Buys you the stated goal with no interface change; costs you nothing
    except the parameter you asked for.

  Worth flagging either way: [38;5;153mlogin()[39m currently [38;5;153mconsole.log[39ms the identity ([38;5;153mapp.js:5[39m), so whatever identifier we add lands
  in browser console logs. If it's PII, that's a data-handling decision, not a formatting one.

  My recommendation is the attempt ID [3mif[0m what you actually want is correlating a login to downstream activity, and
  option 3 if you just want a record of who logged in — but you know which of those it is and I don't.
